RULE OF THUMB (P<1e-3, rho = 0.025):  bits of freedom allowed  b_max = log2(0.02/delta)
   delta 1e-3 -> 4.3 bits (about ONE free integer in 1..12 and nothing else);  1e-5 -> 11.0 bits;  1e-9 -> 24.3 bits (about 6.8 integers in 1..12);  CODATA limit 1.6e-10 -> 26.9 bits
   costs: an integer in 1..12 = 3.6 bits; an integer in 1..100 = 6.6 bits; a choice among 5 operations = 2.3 bits; a choice among 3 constants = 1.6 bits; a unary decoration (5 options) = 2.3 bits;
   each extra 'scale of alpha' or 'group/dimension/normalisation' you might have tried multiplies N (adds log2 of its count).
   So: a 1e-3 match is evidence only if the whole family has <= ~20 members; a 1e-5 match, <= ~2000; a 1e-9 match, <= ~2e7 (E2-size); and NO numeric match can support a family larger than ~1e8 (27 bits), because the
   data stop at 1.6e-10. Evidence must come from FORCING the choices (N -> 1), not from a hit.

